    "result_text": "All 16 tests pass. **BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed telephone handling to store and compare numbers in **E.164** form.\n\n**New shared utility** \u2014 `rest/function/owner/TelephoneE164.java`:\n- `toE164(raw)`: keeps a leading `+` and its country code when present; otherwise assumes `+61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits. Strips spaces, dashes, brackets (all non-digits). Returns `null` when no digits.\n- `isValid(e164)`: requires 8\u201315 digits after the `+`.\n\n**`NormalizeOwnerTelephone`** now converts the create-owner body to E.164, storing the `+\u2026` string as `telephone`, and throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400) when it can't form valid E.164.\n\n**`CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique`** now compares the request's E.164 value against each existing owner's telephone re-normalized to E.164, so national and international spellings of the same number collide (\u2192 409).\n\n**`Owner` entity** \u2014 replaced the `@Digits(integer=10)` + `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` constraints (which rejected the stored `+\u2026` value and caused a 500) with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")`, and removed the now-unused `@Digits` import.\n\n**`InvalidTelephoneException`** message updated to describe the E.164 rule.\n\nVerified against the spec's examples: `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, `+61 412 345 678` collides with the national form, and `12` is rejected with 400.",
